you  [Music]  [Music]  rhyme feels a little like someone  telling you the only way you can see the  Mona Lisa is by unlocking a really heavy  safe the painting is beautiful sure but  my god why did you make seeing it so  bloody difficult although rhyme looks  and sounds gorgeous it's visual splendor  is locked behind frustrating shallow  puzzles and a confusing story meaning  you spend more time figuring out where  to go than taking in the world around  you  much like journey or Eiko rhyme features  no text and only a basic unfamiliar  language your blank slate child  character communicates through  nondescript calls and facial expressions  similarly there's no hints or heads up  this way and ingame cues are portrayed  through abstract audio-visual signals  such as cave paintings and animal cries  [Music]  when the game opens with your character  waking up on an unfamiliar Island the  absence of these typical gameplay themes  lends rhymes environment a sense of  mystery you'll wonder where you are how  you got there and what you're supposed  to be doing and you'll want to explore  the island to discover the answers this  is Ryan at its best the first of four  worlds is a mini sandbox of places I  wanted to go animals I wanted to pet and  objects I wanted to touch the wind in  the trees and the distant sound of  wildlife in addition to a slow classical  soundtrack and no enemies or time  pressure makes it a pleasant relaxing  experience a bit like taking a walk  around a summer park  [Music]  in one beautiful sequence you have to  illuminate a caves darkened floor  beneath you using your singing voice at  times rhyme is magical  before long you'll come across the  game's first puzzles which mostly  involves shouting to release bursts of  energy that activate platforms and doors  these puzzles are expanded upon later in  the game as light manipulation and  pressure plates are added into the mix  but even when more mechanics are added  the puzzles remain simplistic to the  point of being shallow the majority  involve figuring out the one action  required to unlock whichever door is  needed to progress maybe you missed a  key item off the beaten track or maybe  it's a matter of trial and error or  maybe you're just overthinking it and  there's a much simpler solution that you  just haven't seen yet one example  - asks you with manipulating a large  stone pillar to cast light-sensitive  switches in shallow to open a cave door  once I triggered a mid puzzle cutscene  featuring a giant enemy bird  I spent a further 15 minutes fiddling  with the pillar only to realize that I'd  not seen a tiny ledge that allowed me to  climb out these hidden solutions make  the process of figuring out the puzzles  frustrating and the one step victory  hallo  Rhymes poor signposting carries through  to its exploration segment which often  left me clueless as to where to head  next Ryan doesn't have any consistent  goals like journeys shining mountain  with which to orientate yourself and my  cluelessness was made worse by the  repetitive world design each level has  its own theme first is a sunny island  the second a sandy desert the third an  abandoned city and the fourth a rainy  abyss but within each world there isn't  much architectural diversity and the  game does little to explore each theme  in interesting ways I was constantly  ambling forward not really knowing where  I was going or why and this is reflected  in the emotional journey that Ryan has  you follow the story is told through a  series of flashbacks but the abstract  nature of both the world and its lack of  language when it was never clear what my  driving force was I was playing for  progresses sake rather than because I  was desperate to turn the next page in a  story I was really engaged in two  threads that rhyme does lay down or a  hooded figure dressed in red and a  strange magical Fox but these are never  properly resolved and the ending is as  confusing as it is pretentious the twist  introduced that the story's close also  comes far too late to inject the  emotional weight the game sorely needed  up until that point there's no real  struggle little bonding time with other  characters no huge sense of loss not  many moments of elation making this  emotional roller coaster more of a  monotonous merry-go-round  [Music]  rhymes artistry is unquestionable each  world is enchanting in its own way from  the naturalistic peace of the first to  the abstract doom of the last its  soundtrack is similarly beautiful  capable of evoke in wonder tension and  fear in equal measure  [Music]  but when compared to its influences like  ICO Shadow of the Colossus and journey  rhyme doesn't hold up too well  consistent navigation problems coupled  with some frustrating puzzles and severe  framerate dips make Ryan feel like a  well dressed tribute act  [Music]  you  [Music]  you

